00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.660
First of all, Knative is a set
of open source building blocks

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.390
that are specifically
kind of oriented

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.970
for building serverless
on top of Kubernetes.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:02:00.040
What that means is that last
July at this very conference

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.110
we have launched Knative
as an open source project.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.070
And we had at the time
four other partners

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.750
who worked with us on that.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.720
We were very much focusing on
those building block concepts,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.780
knowing that people can pick
and choose those components that

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:14.440
are needed.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.360
But we also saw a
lot of commonality

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.340
that people were working
on in the community.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.970
And while they were very
interesting and complex,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.220
they were also not
very differentiating.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:29.020
So people were often
not being able to get

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:33.260
as user-friendly experience
building service on Kubernetes

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.110
as [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.770
So we launched this.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.160
It's on GitHub under
Apache 2 license.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.410
And right from the start
we focused specifically

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.390
on three areas initially.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.620
But the space is growing.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.330
We focus on building, which
how do you get to an image

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.550
that you can actually
use on Kubernetes

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.670
that would be friendly for
serverless type of workloads.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.510
We focus on eventing,
which we're going

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:57.760
to be talking about today.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.692
And we focused on
serving, which is clearly

00:02:59.692 --> 00:03:01.900
how you're going to be
accessing the serverless stock

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:02.900
at that time.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.660
So very much still focusing on
these modern type of workloads.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:13.660
What that means is that if you
have a stateful application,

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.210
this might not be a
good fit for Knative.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.820
But if you're working in a
kind of modern stock 12 factor

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.430
applications,
microservices, this

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.580
is very much kind of
geared towards delivering

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.830
the best experience of
developing on top of Kubernetes

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:27.620
for you.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:33.360
It also includes 10-plus years
of building serverless stock

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.790
at Google.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:36.580
So all the learnings
from those products

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.190
are kind of applying
here, both from Google

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.980
as well as number of
partners in a community.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.380
So that's pretty exciting.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.080
So very quickly I'm
going to overview

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.300
each one of those components.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.030
So we had a serving built in
events for Serving and Build

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.020
very quickly.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.490
So a couple benefits the
developers get from Knative

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.680
today.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.500
First of all, the one
that we always stress

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.270
is this automatic auto-scaling.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.600
Basically, you
deploy the workload,

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.730
and we take care of this
underlining auto-scaling

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:11.330
based on requests or
usage of that workload.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.560
Not on something like CPU, which
might be more traditional way

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.930
of auto-scaling for Kubernetes.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.579
And what that means
is that we can

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.370
auto-scale through
activation when

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.020
their request is being made.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.480
And then we also can
scale it down to 0

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.550
when that workload
is not being used,

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.600
which is very helpful for
obvious utilization reasons.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.340
So if you have a
Kubernetes cluster,

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.490
that cluster can be used
to provide other services

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.940
or resources that can be
managed through other workloads.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.130
The other very interesting
part for Serving

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.700
is that we kind of
from day one focused

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:51.280
on looking at deployments as
a combination of configuration

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.670
and the actual code.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.520
So it goes in unit,
we freeze that.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.420
We identify it.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.800
We manage it for you.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.280
And what that means that at any
given time you can roll back--

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.410
you can deploy a new
version and roll back

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.480
to the last previous
known state knowing

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.490
that it will go with
the configuration that

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:09.430
was true at the time.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.280
If you've been
building applications,

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.517
the very common pattern
that we see over and over

00:05:14.517 --> 00:05:16.100
is you come to a
conference like this.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.550
You've seen some new language,
new framework, new service.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.600
You get excited.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.650
You build that service.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.040
It goes live.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.450
You startling get users.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:24.070
Yay!

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.570
It's exciting.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:27.460
And now you find out
that there is a bug.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:28.660
Well, I would.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:29.860
You write perfect code.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.830
So that would not be the case
for your code, obviously.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.880
And now you need to
update that application.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.950
But you have users
who constantly use it.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.760
So one of the things that
Knative enables you to do

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.770
is provide a way to manage
that and provides patterns

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.660
like blue/green, which
allows you to shadow

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.600
a new version behind and switch
the traffic to a new one,

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.360
and also use some kind
of experiment framework

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.370
that will allow you to
incrementally switch

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:56.200
the traffic to a new version.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.380
Switch the traffic to a version
that's not exposed to the users

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.980
with a name route access it,
or any combination in between.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.690
So a lot of good
learnings there.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.280
The access control is the
last one I list there.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.730
But this year just highlights.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.220
So obviously we can go deeper
on that at any given time.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.830
There's a lot of documentation.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.410
So this deals with
the fact that you

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.510
might have-- if you're
building microservices,

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.192
sooner or later you
end up with 10, 20,

00:06:19.192 --> 00:06:20.650
and if you're doing
it for a while,

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:22.690
it's going to be hundreds
of microservices.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:24.190
And only one or
two of those need

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.940
to be externally accessible
with the request access path

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.140
control that's integrated.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:33.370
Based on Istio in Knative,
you can manage that

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:37.103
by not exposing routes to
services that don't have

00:06:37.103 --> 00:06:38.270
to be externally accessible.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.580
So it's very helpful.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.150
On a Build side,
right from the day one

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.840
we were focused on delivering
reproducible builds.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.060
That comes very
critical if you want

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.020
to be able to assure that
the same version of code

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.350
built multiple times can
result in the exact same image.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:00.280
And it also enables
us to start kind

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:04.600
of exposing through Build
templates ways for people

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:07.060
to optimize different frameworks
and different languages

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.120
for Build pipelines that allow
developers to go directly

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.210
from source all
the way to a URL.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.290
So yes, there is an
image in between there.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.920
But the developer
doesn't have to see it.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.560
Eliminates a lot of pain
around cross-compiling.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.600
Like number of flags, I
know like in Go is not

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:23.350
too complicated.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:26.470
But if you go to Java, you want
to compile from Java on a Mac

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.830
to Java on Linux, you might
have some different challenges

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:30.480
there.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.280
And also removes
the need for Docker

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.030
entirely on your
workstation, because it's

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:35.770
built on the service.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.570
You send your Build job.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:38.320
It compiles it.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.940
It builds that image for you
and handles the entire thing.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.930
And because it's a cloud scene,
it runs on top of Kubernetes,

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.550
you have access
to all the caching

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.430
and all the elasticity of that
framework of that service.

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:52.103
And you can leverage big
VMs for the build process.

00:07:52.103 --> 00:07:53.770
And you can decommission
them afterwards

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:58.720
and use other nodes that maybe
are more optimized for bursting

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.250
type of workloads.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:02.380
So that's excellent.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:04.418
Very quickly, this is
just high level overview,

00:08:04.418 --> 00:08:05.960
because for the rest
of this session,

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.050
we're going to go deep
into the eventing.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:09.670
So eventing more
than anything is

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:13.060
all about orchestrating the
actual events on your cluster,

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:15.580
both for the event sources
that happen externally

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:17.830
to the cluster, as well as
if they happen on a cluster

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:18.890
itself.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.082
One of the things that we
also focus from day one

00:08:21.082 --> 00:08:23.290
is making sure that that
experience for the developer

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:24.610
is very declarative.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.260
So you don't go in,
run some commands

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.020
that then suddenly you have
to rerun if the notes change.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:32.140
But you tell us the
state that you want.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.850
And using the same
principle that's

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.909
applicable to the
entire Kubernetes stack,

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:41.409
you get that event
registered and bound for you.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:43.822
Higher level objects made it
much easier to reason about.

00:08:43.822 --> 00:08:45.280
So you can kind of
always come back

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.250
and interrogate you
configuration to understand

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:49.660
what exactly has happened.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:51.723
Even though you might
have set it up two weeks,

00:08:51.723 --> 00:08:52.390
three weeks ago.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:55.610
And because it's built
on top of those--

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.630
Knative events are
built on top of serving,

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.880
you continue getting access
to that dynamic scalability.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:04.150
So it allows you to
build this very simple

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.910
triggered demonstration we're
going to show in a minute

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:11.290
here when you wire some kind
of event directly to a service,

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:14.380
and just fire it automatically
all the way to a very

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:15.520
complex pipeline.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.980
And every day I'm
amazed by what people

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:22.000
are building-- channels,
triggers, filters, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.950
all kinds of stuff.

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:28.750
All of that is based on open
standard for event transport.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:32.560
So cloud events is what we
utilize inside of Knative.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.300
And on the end of this, on
the end of the day-- sorry--

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:37.150
is extendable.

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.370
So what that means
is that, yes, we

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.460
have I would say about 20
different sources that already

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.740
publish on the
Knative website that

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.943
are built by the community.

00:09:46.943 --> 00:09:47.860
You can consume those.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:48.700
You don't have to build this.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:49.450
But you know what?

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:52.360
If one of those that
you need is not there,

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.720
you can always come back
and build a new one.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.870
And Ville is going to show
you later how relatively easy

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:01.880
that can be for different
kind of use cases.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:06.090
So without further ado,
let's talk about eventing.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.620
VILLE AIKAS: Thanks, Mark.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:09.650
All right.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:10.460
So yeah.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.710
So I'm really excited to go
ahead and be here and talk

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.570
about the new eventing model.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:17.480
We have spent several
months in looking

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:21.350
at the use cases, how people
use the objects that we

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.640
put together in the--

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:25.880
I don't know-- .2.3 release,
and getting a lot of feedback

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:28.670
from the users on those, and
hearing it loud and clear

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:32.180
that they are pretty
tricky to put together.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:33.845
When we started
the serving stat,

00:10:33.845 --> 00:10:35.720
it was a little bit
easier because Kubernetes

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:37.610
had a lot of primitives
that we could use.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.530
And they lent
themselves quite well

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.420
for building the serving stack.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.100
For the eventing, not so much.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.200
So we had to do a lot of
work on there making sure

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.325
that those primitives are
right so that we can then

00:10:47.325 --> 00:10:50.790
build something really
neat on top of that.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.330
So we are going to talk
about the two new objects

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:54.968
that we introduced.

00:10:54.968 --> 00:10:56.510
If you have used
the eventing before,

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:59.370
there are other things in there.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:01.550
But these are the two new
ones that we talk about,

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.460
because we think they make
the most sense as a user

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:06.750
to go ahead and reason about
the state of your system

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.570
and to make it useful.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:14.000
So really the developer cares
about one thing and one thing

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.680
only, which is to
go and say, I want

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.110
to go ahead and take
events like this.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.310
And I want to send
them over here.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:23.280
If you think about it like
that, that makes sense to me.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.350
So you don't really care about
where those events came from.

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:27.220
Right?

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:29.845
That should be a detail that you
shouldn't have to worry about.

00:11:29.845 --> 00:11:31.928
You're just like, I just
want to go ahead and open

00:11:31.928 --> 00:11:34.140
an object that's uploaded
to my storage, for example.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:36.110
And when that happens,
do the thumb mailer.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.330
The classic example.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:42.500
But you do, however, care
about how these events show up

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.370
in your function.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.620
And as Mark mentioned, we
are using Cloud events.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:48.920
So it's a CNCF spec
that a lot of companies

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.610
are working on,
including Google.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:53.090
So we are standing on the
shoulders of the great work

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:54.720
that other people
have been doing.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.420
So we want to go and
emphasize the fact

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:57.837
that you as the
function developer

00:11:57.837 --> 00:12:00.860
can go ahead and have a standard
way of consuming these events.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:03.890
So you go and get
the cloud events.

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:06.320
And by using these two
new models with the broker

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:08.780
and trigger, we are really
unifying the consumption, which

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.238
is where you say,
hey, I would like

00:12:10.238 --> 00:12:12.980
to go and receive these events
and put them over here--

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:14.430
a function or whatever.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:19.370
And then it also unifies
the event production side

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:20.160
of things.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.160
And we'll talk a little
bit more about that one.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:25.250
But there's a
really nice model so

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:26.960
that you can think
about and reason

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.800
about the system
at the high level.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:31.730
So let's look at these two
objects a little bit more

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:32.850
in detail.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:36.170
So developers are probably
going to use this resource

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.900
the most, which
is really allowing

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.690
them to go ahead and say, hey,
when this event type happens,

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:45.920
please call this
function over here.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.300
Today-- so some examples
of this would be like,

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.980
hey, when a PR is
put into a GitHub,

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:53.970
then do some things with that.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:54.470
Right?

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:58.720
And we use this in
the Knative repo.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.440
We have many of these
little robots that

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:02.780
do things like fix
spelling mistakes

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.090
or go ahead and make
sure that yaml looks

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:07.200
right and things like that.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.700
So we are using
this in the Knative,

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:11.310
and it's really, really neat.

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:12.733
So that's a good
example for that.

00:13:12.733 --> 00:13:14.150
Or you might want
to say, hey, you

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:19.370
know when objects
are loaded into GCS,

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:21.820
I want to go and receive
those, but only certain kinds.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:25.440
So that's the concept
of the filtering there.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.920
So the other nice thing
is that in this model,

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:29.630
the trigger or
your function code

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:32.670
can actually go ahead and
just return a new event type.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.190
So if you, for example, want
to go ahead and say like,

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.420
hey, I want to take
some kind of an event.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.563
But it might contain some
sensitive information

00:13:40.563 --> 00:13:42.230
that I don't want
everybody else to see.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:44.750
You can go ahead and either
create a new event or event

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.110
type, and then you can go and
feed it back in the system.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:51.410
So it makes the function
writer's job much, much easier

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.040
on that sense.

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:55.590
So in this particular one, for
example, we are saying, hey,

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:58.850
I want to go and receive events
from com.example.some event.

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:01.670
And we are sending
it to a subscriber.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.100
And if you're familiar
with Kubernetes or Knative,

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.230
that is basically
an object graph.

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:12.830
In this case, it is using
a service that's serving.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.680
The other new event
is the event broker.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.440
And this is really, I
personally think of it

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.440
as the event delivery
system or the mesh.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:25.520
So when you come into a
namespace of your choosing,

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.740
and you decide that you want
to sprinkle some Knative

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:31.130
eventing on it, you can just
go ahead and apply a label

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:32.360
on that namespace.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:35.150
And the Knative will
install a broker for you

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:36.560
in that namespace.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.180
And as a side
effect of that, you

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:42.710
will now get a discoverable
endpoint/predictable endpoint

00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:44.570
so that you can go
ahead now target

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:47.480
that event mesh from
any other namespace

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:51.320
or from the same
namespace as well.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:56.540
So you can also install
multiple of these as well

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:58.922
into your namespace on
multiple namespaces.

00:14:58.922 --> 00:15:01.130
And you might say, well,
why would I want to do that?

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:04.710
And really there's a couple
of different reasons.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.500
But one of the
things that happens

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:08.010
underneath the
scenes for the broker

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:12.470
is the task to manage
some messaging layers.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:15.170
So these are the channels, if
you've been using the Knative

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:16.570
eventing in the past.

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:19.025
And you might want to go
ahead and choose a couple

00:15:19.025 --> 00:15:20.150
of different broker models.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:21.550
So on one of them you
might want to say,

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:23.260
I don't want to lose
any of these events.

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:26.050
I'm willing to go and pay
the cost of processing them

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:28.122
into the persistent messaging.

00:15:28.122 --> 00:15:29.830
And the other one is
where you might just

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.245
be doing some you don't care.

00:15:31.245 --> 00:15:32.620
And you might
choose to go and do

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.390
things like in-memory
implementation

00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:36.090
where if an event
gets lost, fine.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:37.510
Rerun it again or whatnot.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:39.470
So you have some options there.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:42.910
But you get it for free if
you just label the namespace.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:46.870
And you can also go and
use the broker object

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:50.590
for controlling access
and ingress policy

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:51.910
into that broker.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:53.560
So you can say, for
example, I don't

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:56.500
want any events that I
have not pre-authorized

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:58.180
to enter this broker.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:01.572
And that's one example of that.

00:16:01.572 --> 00:16:04.030
The other one is where you're
doing some cheap and cheerful

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:05.500
testing, and you just
want to do stuff,

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.500
produce events willy
nilly and everything else.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:11.220
So good luck with that.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:13.840
The last one that
didn't unfortunately

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:16.510
make it to 0.5, but I just
wanted to point out here

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:21.490
is that this is the registry
that we are working refining

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:23.500
as a community right
now, making sure it's

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.215
nice, nice and usable.

00:16:25.215 --> 00:16:27.340
And really, it's meant to
solve the use case of how

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:28.670
do you discover the event type.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:31.565
So once you're in your broker
namespace or in your namespace,

00:16:31.565 --> 00:16:33.190
you want to be able
to have an easy way

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:35.110
to discover what
event types there are,

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:37.480
what do they look
like, and so forth.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:41.440
So this is something that the
registry is going to solve.

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:44.520
And we are working very hard to
get it into the next release.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:50.350
So this is what it
really looks like.

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:53.390
Or at least how I see the world.

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:55.890
So if you think about from
the left-hand side there,

00:16:55.890 --> 00:17:00.640
we have two event sources
that are processing events.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:03.105
And they both--
so there's the red

00:17:03.105 --> 00:17:04.480
and then there's
the blue source.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:06.190
And they're both
pushing those events

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:11.109
into the broker where the broker
is happily receiving them.

00:17:11.109 --> 00:17:14.410
And then a developer comes in
on the right-hand side there

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.410
and defines one of those
triggers where they

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:17.890
come in and saying, like, hey.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:20.410
I am actually interested
in the red events.

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:22.240
So you create a
trigger, but you specify

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:23.829
that the filtering is red.

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:27.819
And now your service gets
called only for the red events.

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:31.300
And on the bottom side you
can probably guess that then

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:33.040
that one gets only
the blue events.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:34.990
Right?

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.110
In both cases of the service
I just highlighted the fact

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:40.900
that it's callable, because
it's using something

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.330
that in the Knative we make
extensive use of to make

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:45.060
it easy to extend unpluggable.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:48.820
It's duck typing concept
that Matt Moore and I have

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:50.865
been working on for a while.

00:17:50.865 --> 00:17:52.240
So the nice thing
is that you can

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:53.490
put different kinds of things.

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:58.120
One of those is a Knative
service from the serving stack.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:04.160
But you can put these things
into other places as well.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.910
So let's go ahead and see what
this looks like in action.

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:09.700
Mark has cooked
up something neat.

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:10.492
MARK CHMARNY: Yeah.

00:18:10.492 --> 00:18:12.740
So before we go there,
a little setup here.

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:15.247
Now this chart looks very--
this diagram looks very complex.

00:18:15.247 --> 00:18:16.580
And we'll kind of break it down.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:18.273
I kind of put a little
line around this

00:18:18.273 --> 00:18:19.940
where are we going
to be focusing today.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.482
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:18:21.482 --> 00:18:23.360
to create a very
simplistic trigger that

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:24.920
will fire on a pops-up event.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:27.710
Pops-up is a very simple
globally distributed

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:30.643
event messaging system
that's easy to consume.

00:18:30.643 --> 00:18:32.060
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:34.393
going to create a simple
trigger that will fire whenever

00:18:34.393 --> 00:18:35.520
a new event arrives.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:37.598
But I wasn't going to
create new events myself.

00:18:37.598 --> 00:18:39.140
So what we're going
to do is actually

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:41.690
going to leverage one of the
events that already generated

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:42.440
by Cloud Build.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.510
If you attended some
of the cloud run demos,

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:46.430
I've used that
demonstration there.

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:47.810
So we will fire simple build.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.930
And whenever the build publishes
a new status of a build,

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:53.670
we'll attach to it
and fire an event.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:56.390
Now events are great,
but not really meaningful

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:57.540
until you can see them.

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.207
So what are we going
to do in this case,

00:18:59.207 --> 00:19:01.670
we're to send this
to Slack in order

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:04.538
to be able to display the
actual content of that.

00:19:04.538 --> 00:19:06.080
So can we switch to
the demo machine?

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:13.455
I'll make sure Slack
is running too.

00:19:13.455 --> 00:19:14.040
All right.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:15.665
So first thing what
we're going to do--

00:19:15.665 --> 00:19:16.500
can you see this?

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:19.510
Yeah.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:20.360
It's pretty good.

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:22.235
So what we're gonna do
is we're going to look

00:19:22.235 --> 00:19:23.640
at the trigger object in here.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.940
So if you've worked
with Kubernetes before,

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:27.710
you should be very
familiar with the fact

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:30.860
that there is some namespace
for the API and the version.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:34.190
In this case, we've created
a new object called Trigger.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:36.260
That trigger allows
us to name it.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.237
And for demo purposes,
I named it pretty long.

00:19:39.237 --> 00:19:40.820
But you can name it
whatever you want.

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:43.760
We really go in with a
lot of defaults in here.

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:48.350
So we're using the
serving namespace

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:50.600
to be able to define
an addressable object.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.325
In this case, our
addressable object

00:19:52.325 --> 00:19:54.950
is going to be our Notification
Service building, the one we're

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:56.690
going to send those events to.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:20:00.440
So let's go and fire this up.

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:02.610
So kubectl apply config.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:05.460
Again, very Kubernetes-friendly
way of doing it.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.580
There is number of CLIs
now that are already

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:11.645
available in the community
for Knative so that--

00:20:11.645 --> 00:20:13.020
if you're coming
from Kubernetes,

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:14.668
you're very familiar
with kubectl.

00:20:14.668 --> 00:20:15.710
You can run this anytime.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:16.210
Right?

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:18.680
Or kubecattle, depends--
there's a battle still

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:20.040
to how to exactly pronounce it.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:21.530
But if you're
familiar with that,

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:22.697
you can continue using that.

00:20:22.697 --> 00:20:25.400
But there is a number of CLIs
available to actually hide

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:28.220
even that from you and allow
you to deal specifically only

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.110
with the Knative objects.

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:33.305
And canticle, or
KNCTL, is one of those.

00:20:33.305 --> 00:20:38.180
Knative project has its own CLI
building being built right now.

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:39.510
So you're welcome to try this.

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:41.990
But in this case, we're going
to go straight to kubectl.

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:44.502
We're going to apply this.

00:20:44.502 --> 00:20:45.460
How's the command line?

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:46.280
Big enough?

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:47.230
Yeah.

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:50.620
So what we've got messages
that the trigger was created.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:52.220
But let's not trust
those messages.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:57.280
Let's run get triggers.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:59.650
And I'm running everything
in a demo namespace

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:01.618
just for isolation purposes.

00:21:01.618 --> 00:21:03.160
But what you will
see here is that we

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:07.555
got the-- let me see if
I can make this bigger.

00:21:07.555 --> 00:21:08.880
There we go.

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:12.890
You got the name, the
status, each trigger has

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:15.200
its internal URL-- obviously,
that's not accessible

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.280
externally-- and
the age in here.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:21.050
So what we've done now is
we've set up the trigger.

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:23.480
That's all I had to do
in order to attach myself

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.140
to the source of events.

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:27.620
So let's generate
some events now.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.850
Very quickly before
we even go there,

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:33.170
let's take a look at
that actual service.

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.610
That service is really
just for demo purposes.

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:38.430
But what it does has a couple
of interesting things here.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:41.030
First of all, there's
a handler that whenever

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:42.403
it receives those cloud events--

00:21:42.403 --> 00:21:44.570
remember, we talked about
the transport always using

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:46.653
cloud events so you can
trust that the type you're

00:21:46.653 --> 00:21:49.357
going to be getting is going
to be [INAUDIBLE] cloud events.

00:21:49.357 --> 00:21:50.940
We're going to get
one of those types.

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:54.270
We're going to extract the
actual message of that.

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:57.590
And then we're going to
send it to a sender that

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:59.750
decorates nice message.

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:02.420
And then at very end, it
just sends it to Slack.

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:05.065
And hopefully, if everything
goes well-- fingers crossed--

00:22:05.065 --> 00:22:07.190
in our demo we're going to
be able to go in and see

00:22:07.190 --> 00:22:08.900
the status being published.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:11.030
So let's go to--

