00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:48.640
So can we change back
to the slides, please?

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:51.708
So back to our channels here.

00:23:51.708 --> 00:23:53.000
Those events could be anything.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.940
Ville was talking about
the number, like, get hops

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:56.110
and pushes and everything.

00:23:56.110 --> 00:23:57.830
The process would
be one and the same.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:00.010
You go in and create a trigger.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:02.410
You attach the trigger to
some kind of event source.

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:05.960
And we will go into the
event sources in a minute.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:08.748
And then you also send who is
the consumer of those events.

00:24:08.748 --> 00:24:10.540
And in this case was
a notification service

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:12.860
that adheres to that interface.

00:24:12.860 --> 00:24:13.790
And we send it.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:14.890
So pretty simple.

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:15.755
Pretty easy.

00:24:15.755 --> 00:24:17.380
There's a number of
those demonstration

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:18.620
in Knative samples.

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.620
And you can kind of
reproduce this pretty easily

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:21.970
on your own Knative cluster.

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:26.950
Whoop.

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:27.950
VILLE AIKAS: Good catch.

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:30.210
All right.

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:33.130
So one of the things that
we didn't talk about yet,

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:34.380
but let's talk about them now.

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:36.870
So we've really focused on what
the developer experience is

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:38.430
there where they can come
in and say, like, hey,

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:38.970
here's my trigger.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:40.210
It was very easy to set up.

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:42.570
You basically just said, I
want those kind of events.

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:44.120
I want them go over here, right?

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:44.880
That was nice.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.070
There was no mention of how
did those events show up

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:47.790
in the cluster.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:49.480
OK.

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:52.963
And it's not magic,
it turns out.

00:24:52.963 --> 00:24:54.130
We haven't figured that out.

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:55.963
So there is some stuff
you have to do to get

00:24:55.963 --> 00:24:57.700
those events into your cluster.

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:03.090
And by utilizing
these event sources,

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:06.170
the user is going to care about
a couple of different things.

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:09.300
They are going to
care about, OK,

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:11.110
how do I actually get
those events then?

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:11.610
Right.

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:13.830
So you want to be able to
discover these event sources.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:15.350
So for example, if
you're like, OK.

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:18.980
I really would like to go ahead
and build my own little GitHub

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:20.132
robot or whatever else.

00:25:20.132 --> 00:25:21.090
So how would I do that?

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:22.880
So you're going to be able to
discover that there is a GitHub

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:24.240
event source out there for you.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:24.947
Right?

00:25:24.947 --> 00:25:27.530
And you also want to be able to
configure those external event

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:30.050
sources, because you have
to be able to do things

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:32.610
like, for example, in the GitHub
you want to be able to say,

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:34.340
I need to go ahead
and watch events

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:38.180
on this particular
bucket owner or repo--

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:39.350
project and repo.

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:41.750
And you want to be able
to specify those ones.

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:44.450
And lastly, you want to
be able to go and say,

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:45.160
but that's great.

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:47.090
But I have my own
internal system.

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:49.860
And I'm sure nobody is going to
write an event source for that.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:52.190
So you always have the
option of writing yourself

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:56.420
and be able to go ahead
and use it internally.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:58.970
But hopefully you
can come in and write

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:02.760
some other external ones
and come play with us.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:07.770
So Knative Event community has
already built many of these.

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:10.715
So here is just a very, very
small sample of the ones.

00:26:10.715 --> 00:26:12.590
But you can see the rich
set of event sources

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:16.760
that you can go and come
in and start using today.

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:19.488
But many of these
are in the repo.

00:26:19.488 --> 00:26:21.530
Some of them are in the
different kinds of repos,

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:24.210
because they are maintained by
different people and so forth.

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:26.540
But on the bottom
there is a link that

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:30.060
contains all the event sources.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:31.610
And if you write
one, and you want

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:33.740
to go ahead and come
in and expose it

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:35.600
to a wider set of
users, then you

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:39.830
can go ahead and add it to
the list and to send out a PR.

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:42.980
So let's go and see what
does it look like to go

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:46.080
and actually go ahead and use
one of these event sources.

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:48.590
So first demo we
talked about, events

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:51.800
were all wired up to
come into the broker.

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:54.680
And all you needed to do was
to go and create a trigger

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:56.070
to go ahead and consume those.

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:58.760
So now let's go and complete the
picture and saying, like, yay.

00:26:58.760 --> 00:26:59.420
OK.

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:00.890
Now let's go to
look at the magic--

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:02.615
which, it's not
magic-- but what does

00:27:02.615 --> 00:27:03.990
that look like
when I go and say,

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:06.020
