With the event
sources, the benefits

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:02.460
of using them in this way
is that it actually means

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:03.620
that they are declarative.

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:04.230
Right?

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:07.227
So you can go ahead and
create these in your cluster.

00:33:07.227 --> 00:33:08.310
You can actually see them.

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:10.060
So as Mark was showing
there, you can say,

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:12.480
what are the subscriptions
that I have going on for me

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:14.310
right now?

00:33:14.310 --> 00:33:16.080
You don't have to go
ahead and remember

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:19.350
how things are done in
the sense that creating

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:22.500
web hooks and various websites,
it's always different,

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:25.960
and it's difficult to remember
how they were created,

00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:28.420
and there's all these
how-to pages, and so forth.

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:30.990
So by using the
event sources you

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:32.610
can do this all declaratively.

00:33:32.610 --> 00:33:35.130
You can check into conflicts
into your source control,

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:36.600
which I'm sure
you are all doing,

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:38.670
because conflict is code.

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:41.440
So you are doing all
the right things.

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:42.750
And they also reconcilable.

00:33:42.750 --> 00:33:44.790
So if you make changes
those event sources,

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:47.010
then all the right things
happen for you underneath.

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.110
So the source is then
responsible for taking

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:52.080
care of that on the
authoritative side.

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:54.180
So that's really, really nice.

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:57.030
And the last bit is that there's
a now very clear separation

00:33:57.030 --> 00:34:00.510
of concerns so that
the broker is basically

00:34:00.510 --> 00:34:02.580
creating a nice little
eventing mesh that

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:03.810
takes in all the events.

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:08.100
And then the user focuses
on routing those events

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:11.460
to the appropriate
functions by using triggers.

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:12.610
So that's nice.

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:14.909
And then on the other side
we have these event sources

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:18.090
that are responsible for
then establishing the control

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:20.909
plane and the data plane and
ensuring that those events are

00:34:20.909 --> 00:34:22.540
now delivered in the broker.

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:25.199
So there's a nice new
clean separation there.

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:30.750
So while the Knative
community has

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.282
provided many of
these event sources

00:34:33.282 --> 00:34:34.949
already, as I mentioned
earlier, there's

00:34:34.949 --> 00:34:37.830
probably some that
we haven't done yet.

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:40.199
I know there's a lot of
them that we haven't done.

00:34:40.199 --> 00:34:42.912
And you might also have
some internal systems.

00:34:42.912 --> 00:34:44.370
Do you want to go
ahead and say, it

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:47.489
would be really nice if that
thing that I already have

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:49.170
would produce events for me.

00:34:49.170 --> 00:34:52.469
And we think that's
saying, great.

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.380
Just rewrite it so that it's
cloud native is probably

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:57.760
not the best answer.

00:34:57.760 --> 00:34:59.820
So we can go ahead
and make sure that we

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:03.060
can take these bits of code
in the form of event sources

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:05.440
and kind of bridge those
two worlds together.

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:08.430
So let's look a
little bit at this

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:11.600
and see what is sort of involved
in building one of these event

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:13.770
sources.

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:16.840
So there's really two concerns
that you have to think about.

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:18.370
So one of them is
the control plane.

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:21.480
So the control plane is
the one where you are

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:24.510
asking some external system--

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:25.480
external to brokers.

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:27.022
When I say "external,"
I don't really

00:35:27.022 --> 00:35:28.980
care whether it's
internal or external.

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:32.280
But I do think that it's
outside of the broker's realm.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:34.990
So it's external to the broker.

00:35:34.990 --> 00:35:36.660
So there's a
control plane action

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:39.660
where you are asking some
system to go ahead and start

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:43.920
sending you events that
are filtered according

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:45.270
to your specification.

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:47.520
So here are some examples.

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:50.190
Creating a GitHub web hook
for a particular repository

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:53.090
and defining what kinds of event
types you might care about.

00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:56.790
So PRs, commits, comments,
and things like that.

00:35:56.790 --> 00:36:00.480
Or creating a GCP PubSub
subscription or GCS

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:02.760
object storage
notification and so forth.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:05.100
I'm sure you can
all hopefully think

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:09.070
of a couple of other systems
that are not on that list.

00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:11.420
And then there is also the
concern about the data plane.

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:13.170
So it's great that you
have asked somebody

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:14.200
to send you events.

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:16.860
But where are they going
to be sent to, right?

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:18.820
So that is the concern
of the data plane.

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:21.090
So in this case, for
example, we can go ahead

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:24.930
and create a data plane
component that receives

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:26.820
the GitHub web hook events.

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:30.330
Or we can go ahead and
have a piece of software

00:36:30.330 --> 00:36:31.950
that is running on
our cluster that

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:33.990
is responsible for
handling pull models,

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:36.180
because there's both
kinds of models out there.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:37.680
So there's push and
pull based ones.

00:36:41.340 --> 00:36:43.590
So on the control plane--

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:46.560
so while on the
broker side of things

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:48.510
we are standardizing
on the cloud events,

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:51.440
and there's a nice little
control plane component there

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:53.000
that allows you to do that.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:54.750
There is nothing like
that, unfortunately,

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:56.580
out there that allows
us to go and have

00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:59.710
a standard way of subscribing
to events everywhere.

00:36:59.710 --> 00:37:02.660
And I don't think that that's
going to happen anytime soon.

00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:03.650
But it may be.

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:04.500
That would be nice.

00:37:04.500 --> 00:37:07.530
But if you look at, for example,
when Mark was demonstrating

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:09.960
the Twitter example,
there was things that he

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:11.670
had to go and specify, like--

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:12.880
I don't know-- credentials.

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:13.380
Right?

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:15.430
So credentials come in
different kinds of format.

00:37:15.430 --> 00:37:17.250
So the control plane
has to be customized

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:20.820
for a particular event so that
it knows how to authenticate

00:37:20.820 --> 00:37:23.290
itself and so forth.

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:26.220
They also all have
different custom APIs.

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:28.072
The nice thing is
that many of these--

00:37:28.072 --> 00:37:30.280
there's many, many libraries
that you can go and pull

00:37:30.280 --> 00:37:34.360
in the form of your
own choosing, language

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:36.050
of your choice.

00:37:36.050 --> 00:37:37.550
And then it also
has to reconcile.

00:37:37.550 --> 00:37:40.420
So I basically-- I mentioned
earlier that if I make changes

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:43.840
to the Twitter example
and reapply those changes,

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:45.580
then the Twitter
source is going to be

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:48.250
responsible for pushing those
and modifying the control plane

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:50.860
bits as necessary.

00:37:50.860 --> 00:37:53.800
And the second bit that the
control plane needs to do

00:37:53.800 --> 00:37:56.470
is as it is setting up the
control plane it obviously

00:37:56.470 --> 00:37:58.360
needs to go and
point it at something

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:01.390
and establish the data plane.

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:04.750
So the event source is also
responsible for setting up

00:38:04.750 --> 00:38:06.640
the data plane components.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:10.695
And a couple of reasons
for that are, well, there's

00:38:10.695 --> 00:38:12.070
probably some
authentication that

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:13.480
has to happen at
the data plane also.

00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:13.780
Right?

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:15.572
So if you, for example,
set up a GitHub web

00:38:15.572 --> 00:38:19.360
hook, then one of the ways
that you can authenticate

00:38:19.360 --> 00:38:21.310
there is by using
a shared secret.

00:38:21.310 --> 00:38:24.370
So the source is going to go
and set up a Brando secret,

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:27.130
give it to GitHub, because
nobody else cares about it,

00:38:27.130 --> 00:38:29.770
gives it to GitHub and
gives it to data plane.

00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:31.480
And then you're
off to the races.

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:32.860
And then obviously,
control plane

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:34.880
needs to go ahead and
clean up after it's done.

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:38.390
So once you decide that maybe
you don't want this anymore,

00:38:38.390 --> 00:38:41.890
it's responsible now for
going in and deleting

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:43.777
the external sources
so that you don't

00:38:43.777 --> 00:38:45.610
have to remember say,
like, oh, I don't want

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:46.810
to receive those events now.

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:48.310
Here's a piece of
documentation that

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:52.370
tells you how to undo all the
stuff that you did before.

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:55.960
So really this just
a simple picture

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:58.060
where the event source
on the left hand--

00:38:58.060 --> 00:39:01.090
the yellow ones, those are
the custom pieces of code

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.132
that run on your cluster.

00:39:03.132 --> 00:39:04.340
So there's two of them there.

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:05.980
There's a GitHub and a GCS.

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:09.160
And both of them are
talking to a control plane.

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:11.860
One of them, the GitHub
API, using the green one.

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:15.430
And one of them
to cloud storage.

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:18.340
And they are just
establishing that data plane

00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:20.183
on the right-hand side
there to the broker.

00:39:20.183 --> 00:39:21.850
That's what they're
conceptually saying.

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:23.510
Please go and send those events.

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:25.990
Send them over to the
broker over there.

00:39:25.990 --> 00:39:27.230
Well, that's nice.

00:39:28.492 --> 00:39:30.700
But then this is where you
have to worry about things

00:39:30.700 --> 00:39:31.825
like the date plane, right?

00:39:31.825 --> 00:39:34.720
So while everything
within Knative eventing

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:38.830
is cloud events, so the broker
says, I only take cloud events.

00:39:38.830 --> 00:39:39.680
Right?

00:39:39.680 --> 00:39:41.720
Nice separation of concerns.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:43.330
So the data plane
is going to have

00:39:43.330 --> 00:39:46.030
to normalize those
events that are coming in

00:39:46.030 --> 00:39:49.270
from the external sources,
convert them into cloud events,

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:53.650
and hand them off
over to the broker.

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:55.120
They also need to,
as I mentioned,

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:58.070
deal with the authentication
and authorization.

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:00.580
So just because
somebody sends you

00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:03.598
an event from the
outside world, you

00:40:03.598 --> 00:40:05.140
want to make sure
that it actually is

00:40:05.140 --> 00:40:06.600
the one that you subscribed to.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:08.767
So that's where the
coordination between the control

00:40:08.767 --> 00:40:11.900
plane of the event source
and the data plane--

00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:15.340
there's some coordination
that needs to happen.

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:17.540
And again, there's
pull versus push.

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:21.997
So some examples
are for GCP PubSub.

00:40:21.997 --> 00:40:23.080
You can run in both modes.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:24.940
You can run it in pull
mode or push mode.

00:40:24.940 --> 00:40:28.390
So depending on how you want to
go ahead and set up your data

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:29.950
plane, you have choices there.

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:34.490
Push base obviously lends itself
very nicely to Knative serving,

00:40:34.490 --> 00:40:37.660
so that many of these event
sources that are push-based

00:40:37.660 --> 00:40:40.930
are implemented using
just a Knative service.

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:45.750
Yes.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:46.470
Yes.

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:49.410
And so.

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:52.470
So it roughly looks like this.

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:56.790
So that we kind of covered
everything about the broker on.

00:40:56.790 --> 00:41:00.360
So the first part was the
control plane-- the yellows.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:02.400
Now here's sort of the
data plane for you.

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:04.350
So you can see that the--

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:06.840
for example, the
FTP receive adapter

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:10.320
that we have
written, for example,

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:12.750
is the one that is pull-based.

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:14.700
Because many of the
FTP servers out there

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:18.360
don't expose a
push-based model, or they

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:19.700
don't expose any notifications.

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:22.782
So you have to go ahead
and do something like this.

00:41:22.782 --> 00:41:24.240
But this is a good
example of being

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:27.900
able to take a little bit of
glue code and saying, you know,

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:29.850
now all of a sudden
without changing anything

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:33.840
on the original source that
you cared about, you can go

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:35.820
and write a little
bit of [? shin ?] code

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:38.940
and go and print those eventing
capabilities now to an existing

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:41.220
system out there.

00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:43.380
And then GitHub and GCS.

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:45.800
So they are all sending
different kinds of events.

00:41:45.800 --> 00:41:50.340
Off they go into their
respective receive adapters.

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:53.700
And out comes nice,
normalized cloud events

00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:56.520
that then get handed
off to the broker.

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:59.320
Obviously, they all contain
different kinds of payloads.

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:01.310
So if you look at the
one on the bottom there,

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:04.230
there's a quick example of
what do these magical cloud

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:05.440
events look like.

00:42:05.440 --> 00:42:08.370
So there's this kind of
standard envelope around it.

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:10.240
And then the payload
goes in the data there.

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:13.290
So it's very easy for
the function writers

00:42:13.290 --> 00:42:15.150
to go ahead and
reason about these.

00:42:15.150 --> 00:42:16.832
MARK CHMARNY: Seven minutes.

00:42:16.832 --> 00:42:17.790
VILLE AIKAS: All right.

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:21.750
So there's basically
three standard base today

00:42:21.750 --> 00:42:23.310
to go and write event sources.

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:27.360
So one of them is the, I want
to go and do it all myself.

00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:29.280
I want to write a
Kubernetes controller,

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:30.840
because that is fun.

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:34.662
And that's a lot of work.

00:42:34.662 --> 00:42:36.870
So we came up with something
called Knative Container

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:37.540
Source.

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:39.260
I'll talk a little
bit about that one.

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:41.010
And then the last bit
is where you already

00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:43.445
have some running code
that you have control over.

00:42:43.445 --> 00:42:44.820
And you want to
go and just start

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:47.890
emitting events from there.

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:52.420
So the Kubernetes
Operator is basically

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:55.090
this is a standard way that
you basically add a new object

00:42:55.090 --> 00:42:56.230
type in the Kubernetes.

00:42:56.230 --> 00:42:57.950
You basically define
a CRD says this

00:42:57.950 --> 00:42:59.675
is what the objects look like.

00:42:59.675 --> 00:43:02.050
And then you write a controller
that sits there and looks

00:43:02.050 --> 00:43:04.490
at those objects and
reconciles the world.

00:43:04.490 --> 00:43:08.560
And then you have
web hook that is

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:11.440
authenticating and
validating and defaulting

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:13.270
and things like that.

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:14.680
So it's only three pieces.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:15.670
But it's a lot of work.

00:43:15.670 --> 00:43:16.330
Trust me.

00:43:16.330 --> 00:43:17.590
I've written a few of these.

00:43:17.590 --> 00:43:18.610
So that's a lot of work.

00:43:18.610 --> 00:43:21.640
And it's very complicated to
get it right and so forth.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:24.370
There's a great example
that actually uses

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:26.423
this with just a GCP PubSub.

00:43:26.423 --> 00:43:28.090
So I'm not going to
go through the code.

00:43:28.090 --> 00:43:29.110
But there it is.

00:43:29.110 --> 00:43:31.423
You can go look at it.

00:43:31.423 --> 00:43:33.340
So what we did at the
Knative, we're like, OK.

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:33.520
Look.

00:43:33.520 --> 00:43:34.562
It's really, really hard.

00:43:34.562 --> 00:43:39.790
So let's go ahead and write one
controller that is easy for you

00:43:39.790 --> 00:43:41.710
to reason about,
because you just

00:43:41.710 --> 00:43:44.020
say, hey, when a
new event type gets

00:43:44.020 --> 00:43:45.170
created-- like the Twitter.

00:43:45.170 --> 00:43:46.962
So good example of this
is the Twitter one.

00:43:46.962 --> 00:43:49.630
So when we showed
you the Twitter one,

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:52.150
the Twitter source is using
the container storage.

00:43:52.150 --> 00:43:55.213
So you basically
just say like, look.

00:43:55.213 --> 00:43:57.130
I don't want to write a
Kubernetes controller.

00:43:57.130 --> 00:43:58.360
I'm just going to
give you a container.

00:43:58.360 --> 00:44:00.190
Can you run this for
me when you go ahead

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:02.800
and establish a
new subscription,

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:04.750
modify it, and delete it.

00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:08.700
So that's what you can do
with the container source.

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:11.950
You just give us a controller--
uh, controller-- container.

00:44:11.950 --> 00:44:13.447
And we run it for you.

00:44:13.447 --> 00:44:15.280
And the nice thing is
it's language neutral.

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:18.820
So whatever code you have,
just wrap it up in a container

00:44:18.820 --> 00:44:20.110
and bring it to us.

00:44:20.110 --> 00:44:21.970
And with this model
you can also go ahead

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:27.370
and it's possible to combine
the control and data plane.

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:33.000
And one example of this is
the Kubernetes event source.

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:37.930
So this is the
Kubernetes event source

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:40.350
that runs on your cluster.

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:43.450
It watches for Kubernetes
events and turns them

00:44:43.450 --> 00:44:45.200
into Knative events for you.

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:48.100
So then you can go and do things
like automate some aspects

00:44:48.100 --> 00:44:51.380
of your Kubernetes cluster
yourself and things like that.

00:44:51.380 --> 00:44:53.080
And there's obviously
more code here.

00:44:53.080 --> 00:44:55.600
But really, really-- and
there's a link to it.

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:57.887
But the only things that
it really cares about,

00:44:57.887 --> 00:44:59.470
it sets up the control
plane on there,

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:02.170
basically uses Kubernetes
client and says,

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:05.800
I want to go ahead and start
watching for event types--

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:07.120
Kubernetes events.

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:08.620
And when those
events happen, please

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:11.110
call this little
function for me.

00:45:11.110 --> 00:45:13.000
And that's the add
event function there.

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:14.920
And the only thing
that function has to do

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:17.350
is take that, wrap it
up in a cloud event,

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:18.730
and ship it off the broker.

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:20.290
And you are done.

00:45:20.290 --> 00:45:26.510
So that is a pretty
simple one to do.

00:45:26.510 --> 00:45:30.170
And then let's say you already
have some code running.

00:45:30.170 --> 00:45:33.390
And this is our super
secure log-in service here,

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:36.622
for example, where the only
thing we want to do is we

00:45:36.622 --> 00:45:37.580
have some running code.

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:38.810
And we have some
places where we would

00:45:38.810 --> 00:45:40.977
like to go ahead and extend
that system by emittting

00:45:40.977 --> 00:45:41.910
some events.

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:46.880
So all we have to do is from
that existing code decide where

00:45:46.880 --> 00:45:48.250
we want to send the events--

00:45:48.250 --> 00:45:53.270
sorry-- when do you want to
emit events and instantiate

00:45:53.270 --> 00:45:54.720
a new Cloud Events client.

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:57.710
So in this case, it's
our own internal events.

00:45:57.710 --> 00:46:00.050
I just wrap it up
in a cloud event.

00:46:00.050 --> 00:46:03.080
And my data is then
whatever you want it to be.

00:46:03.080 --> 00:46:04.110
Right?

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:05.870
So those are the
kind of three ways

00:46:05.870 --> 00:46:11.780
of doing it in the order of
decreasing difficulty, if you

00:46:11.780 --> 00:46:13.650
will.

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:15.280
And there's a cloud events SDK.

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:18.590
There's one good example,
the cloud events repo,

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:21.180
as I mentioned the spec
theories say that's a Go

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:22.850
lang, because this is Go Lang.

00:46:22.850 --> 00:46:25.670
But you can use that, so
it's pretty easy for you

00:46:25.670 --> 00:46:28.790
to go ahead and do this
yourself if you want to.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:33.642
MARK CHMARNY: All right.

00:46:33.642 --> 00:46:34.850
We've barely made it on time.

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:38.530
But hopefully we gave you a good
overview of the Knative events

00:46:38.530 --> 00:46:40.020
and the capabilities they're in.

00:46:40.020 --> 00:46:42.940
It's very hard to kind of go
super deep but also fit it

00:46:42.940 --> 00:46:44.240
all in an hour and level set.

00:46:44.240 --> 00:46:46.740
There is a lot of examples that
are available on the Knative

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:47.240
website.

00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:50.350
If you go to our Knative.dev
and drill down into samples,

00:46:50.350 --> 00:46:53.410
you will see all different
simple events all the way

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:54.830
to very complex ones.

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:56.590
And you can kind
of pace yourself.

00:46:56.590 --> 00:47:00.062
We also have walked through
some of the spectrum of the way

00:47:00.062 --> 00:47:01.270
you can interact with events.

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:02.920
You can start with
very simplistic one

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:05.500
where it might not be able
to reconcile your sources,

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:08.480
and migrate over time to
something more complex.

00:47:08.480 --> 00:47:10.120
Again, a lot of
those event sources

00:47:10.120 --> 00:47:11.203
have already been written.

00:47:11.203 --> 00:47:12.910
So you don't have to
be in that business

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:14.890
unless you don't want
to-- unless you want to.

00:47:14.890 --> 00:47:17.860
So hopefully this was
good informative session.

00:47:17.860 --> 00:47:20.320
There's a couple links in here
where you can get hold of us

00:47:20.320 --> 00:47:24.370
as well as ask some deeper
questions on a Knative slack.

00:47:24.370 --> 00:47:27.280
It's just
accessKnative.slack.com

00:47:27.280 --> 00:47:29.440
That will redirect to
to the Slack directly.

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:31.300
And you can ask the
different channels

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:35.560
for eventing, community, the
general one, which always good

00:47:35.560 --> 00:47:36.880
place to start with.

00:47:36.880 --> 00:47:41.520
And obviously, this week we have
kind of announced Cloud Run.

00:47:41.520 --> 00:47:44.560
Cloud Run doesn't
support cloud events yet

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:45.610
for this kind of service.

00:47:45.610 --> 00:47:48.760
But as you can imagine, we are
looking forward to the time

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:50.810
where we can offer
that service too.

00:47:50.810 --> 00:47:52.370
Thank you very much.

00:47:52.370 --> 00:47:54.770
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:54.770 --> 00:47:58.420
